vo_avfoundation: show subtitles in the colour of their video

Subtitle colours are the video's RGB: VSFilter blends subtitles into the
picture before it is converted for the display, and typesetters match signs
and masks to the picture in that RGB. Overlay samples carried no colour,
and macOS's compositor shows an untagged buffer as it is while it converts
the video from the colour the video carries: Apple's BT.709 transfer lifts
the picture's midtones (grey 128 shows as 139 on an sRGB display) and not
the subtitle's, and a wide gamut display takes the subtitle's sRGB values
as its own primaries. A mask matched to the picture showed as a patch over it
(plezy#3072, a MacBook Pro's P3 display: a mask over a cyan sketch book
showed as P3 176,242,233, its own values, the book around it as
196,242,237).

Every overlay sample now carries the colour of the video it is shown over:
the colour space, primaries, transfer, gamma and ICC profile of the frame's
buffer as decoded or uploaded, taken again whenever the frame's params
change. flip_page makes the video sample before the OSD's samples so they
have it; the order they are enqueued in is unchanged. The Core Image
composite (video geometry in a composite mode) takes the subtitle image in
the same colour. PQ, HLG and Dolby Vision video keep an untagged overlay:
their transfer would put subtitle white at the signal's peak. The Y'CbCr
composite already blends subtitle RGB with the video's matrix.

macOS 27, a 1080p sRGB display, libmpv drawing into a display layer set up
as Plezy's macOS player sets it up, paused on six colour bands with an ASS
box of each band's RGB over half of it; the largest difference between a
band and its box in displayed 8-bit values, before -> after:

  H.264 BT.709, VideoToolbox                      11 -> 3
  H.264 BT.709, software decoding                 11 -> 3
  HEVC Main 10 BT.709, VideoToolbox               10 -> 3
  composite-osd with video-zoom (Core Image)      11 -> 3
  composite-osd (Y'CbCr blend, unchanged)          3 -> 3
  H.264 untagged, software decoding               11 -> 3

The 3 left is the test file's: its bands decode 2-3 below the RGB they were
made from (grey to 125), the boxes are exact.

iOS 27, an iPhone Air (P3 display), the same bands in a layer set up as
Plezy's iOS player sets it up: iOS shows BT.709 video without macOS's lift,
so the inline overlay matched already (3), and its screenshots are the same
pixel for pixel after; composite-osd with video-zoom 11 -> 3.

